1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,772
July second, nineteen thirty seven.

2
00:00:02,992 --> 00:00:08,331
Somewhere over the Pacific, the most famous pilot in the world is talking on the radio.

3
00:00:08,831 --> 00:00:12,497
She says she must be right on top of the island she's looking for.

4
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:13,815
But she can't see it.

5
00:00:14,035 --> 00:00:15,735
And her gas is running low.

6
00:00:16,235 --> 00:00:18,585
An hour later, the radio goes quiet.

7
00:00:18,805 --> 00:00:19,486
Forever.

8
00:00:19,986 --> 00:00:28,486
Nearly ninety years, dozens of expeditions, and several million dollars later, people are still arguing about where she went.

9
00:00:29,886 --> 00:00:35,259
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is Amelia Earhart.

10
00:00:35,759 --> 00:00:37,146
Everyone knows the name.

11
00:00:37,366 --> 00:00:40,164
Fewer people know the actual radio logs.

12
00:00:40,384 --> 00:00:43,513
And the radio logs are where this story really lives.

13
00:00:44,013 --> 00:00:50,819
So let's follow the flight, the calls, and the clues, and see which theories survive contact with the evidence.

14
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:56,117
By nineteen thirty seven, Amelia Earhart was a superstar.

15
00:00:56,337 --> 00:01:02,910
In nineteen thirty two she became the first woman to fly solo, nonstop, across the Atlantic.

16
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:05,090
Now she wanted something bigger.

17
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:09,184
A flight around the world, as close to the equator as possible.

18
00:01:09,404 --> 00:01:11,462
The longest route anyone had tried.

19
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:20,130
Her navigator was Fred Noonan, an experienced ocean navigator who had helped plan Pan American's routes across the Pacific.

20
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:28,060
Their plane was a Lockheed Electra, a sleek, twin engine, silver aircraft, packed with extra fuel tanks.

21
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:35,826
They flew east, from California, across the United States, South America, Africa, Asia and down to New Guinea.

22
00:01:36,046 --> 00:01:38,007
About twenty two thousand miles.

23
00:01:38,507 --> 00:01:40,347
Seven thousand miles to go.

24
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:42,687
All of it over the Pacific Ocean.

25
00:01:42,907 --> 00:01:46,550
The scariest part was saved for last, like a horror movie.

26
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,158
The next leg was the hardest of all.

27
00:01:50,378 --> 00:01:53,071
From Lae, in New Guinea, to Howland Island.

28
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:56,525
About two thousand five hundred miles of open water.

29
00:01:57,025 --> 00:01:58,806
And Howland Island is tiny.

30
00:01:59,026 --> 00:02:04,413
Just over a mile long, half a kilometer wide, and only around three meters high.

31
00:02:04,913 --> 00:02:10,016
Finding it was like flying across an entire ocean to land on a parking lot.

32
00:02:10,516 --> 00:02:19,372
To help, the US Coast Guard stationed a ship next to the island, the cutter Itasca, to talk to the plane by radio and guide her in.

33
00:02:20,772 --> 00:02:29,134
On July second, the Electra took off from Lae at around ten in the morning, heavy with about eleven hundred gallons of fuel.

34
00:02:29,634 --> 00:02:32,012
The flight would take about eighteen hours.

35
00:02:32,232 --> 00:02:44,025
Most of it at night, navigating by the stars and by dead reckoning, which basically means: we've been going this fast, in this direction, for this long, so we must be about here.

36
00:02:44,525 --> 00:02:50,621
Over thousands of miles, even a tiny error in that calculation grows into a big miss.

37
00:02:52,021 --> 00:02:56,047
On board the Itasca, radio operators waited through the night.

38
00:02:56,547 --> 00:02:57,864
They could hear Earhart.

39
00:02:58,084 --> 00:03:01,503
Her voice came in, sometimes faint, sometimes strong.

40
00:03:01,723 --> 00:03:03,044
But there was a problem.

41
00:03:03,544 --> 00:03:04,971
She didn't seem to hear them.

42
00:03:05,471 --> 00:03:10,309
The plane and the ship were partly using different radio setups and frequencies.

43
00:03:10,529 --> 00:03:14,074
The ship's direction finder couldn't get a fix on her signal.

44
00:03:14,294 --> 00:03:20,373
And there may even have been confusion about which time zone everyone was using for their schedules.

45
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:26,719
In other words, a phone call where both people are shouting hello, and neither can hear the other.

46
00:03:28,119 --> 00:03:32,132
At seven forty two in the morning, the clearest message came through.

47
00:03:32,632 --> 00:03:34,645
We must be on you, but cannot see you.

48
00:03:34,865 --> 00:03:36,416
But gas is running low.

49
00:03:36,636 --> 00:03:38,882
Been unable to reach you by radio.

50
00:03:39,102 --> 00:03:41,289
We are flying at one thousand feet.

51
00:03:41,789 --> 00:03:46,653
The Itasca's crew was pumping black smoke into the sky so she could spot them.

52
00:03:46,873 --> 00:03:47,840
It didn't help.

53
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:51,032
At eight forty three came her last transmission.

54
00:03:51,252 --> 00:03:54,551
We are on the line one five seven, three three seven.

55
00:03:54,771 --> 00:03:56,700
Running on line north and south.

56
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,314
That message is the most important clue in the case.

57
00:04:01,534 --> 00:04:02,783
It's a compass line.

58
00:04:03,003 --> 00:04:09,507
Noonan would have calculated that Howland was somewhere on a line running north northwest to south southeast.

59
00:04:10,007 --> 00:04:13,782
So they were flying up and down that line, looking for the island.

60
00:04:14,002 --> 00:04:17,115
The trouble is, they didn't say where on the line they were.

61
00:04:17,615 --> 00:04:24,837
Follow that line south, about three hundred and fifty nautical miles from Howland, and you hit another island.

62
00:04:25,057 --> 00:04:26,230
Gardner Island.

63
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:28,178
Today called Nikumaroro.

64
00:04:28,678 --> 00:04:29,835
Keep that in mind.

65
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:38,129
The search that followed was the biggest and most expensive air and sea search in US history up to that point.

66
00:04:38,629 --> 00:04:46,283
Navy ships, an aircraft carrier, planes, and the Coast Guard covered hundreds of thousands of square miles of ocean.

67
00:04:46,503 --> 00:04:50,826
It cost around four million dollars, in nineteen thirty seven money.

68
00:04:51,326 --> 00:04:52,417
They found nothing.

69
00:04:52,637 --> 00:04:55,155
No plane, no oil slick, no wreckage.

70
00:04:55,655 --> 00:04:58,193
The search was called off on July nineteenth.

71
00:04:58,413 --> 00:05:02,928
In nineteen thirty nine, Amelia Earhart was declared legally dead.

72
00:05:04,328 --> 00:05:04,914
So.

73
00:05:05,134 --> 00:05:06,190
Where did they go?

74
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:10,646
There are three main theories, and they get progressively more dramatic.

75
00:05:12,046 --> 00:05:13,824
Theory one, the boring one.

76
00:05:14,044 --> 00:05:21,793
They never found Howland, ran out of fuel, ditched in the ocean, and the plane sank in water thousands of meters deep.

77
00:05:22,293 --> 00:05:27,065
This is what the US government concluded, and what most historians think.

78
00:05:27,285 --> 00:05:31,394
It fits the fuel, the radio calls, and the total lack of wreckage.

79
00:05:31,894 --> 00:05:38,043
The problem with this theory is that it requires finding a small plane on the bottom of the Pacific.

80
00:05:38,263 --> 00:05:40,431
Several deep sea searches have tried.

81
00:05:40,651 --> 00:05:42,460
So far, the ocean is winning.

82
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:44,811
Theory two.

83
00:05:45,031 --> 00:05:52,368
They followed that one five seven line south, and landed on the reef at Gardner Island, now Nikumaroro.

84
00:05:52,868 --> 00:06:03,945
In nineteen forty, a British colonial officer on the island found part of a human skeleton, along with what looked like a woman's shoe and a box for a navigation tool.

85
00:06:04,445 --> 00:06:09,053
The bones were examined by a doctor, who decided they belonged to a man.

86
00:06:09,273 --> 00:06:13,056
And then, in the great tradition of history, the bones were lost.

87
00:06:13,556 --> 00:06:23,328
Decades later, a researcher re-analysed the doctor's old measurements and argued they were actually a closer match to Earhart than to most people.

88
00:06:23,548 --> 00:06:25,082
Other experts disagree.

89
00:06:25,302 --> 00:06:27,822
Without the bones, nobody can test them.

90
00:06:28,322 --> 00:06:34,470
Expeditions to Nikumaroro have found intriguing objects, but nothing that clearly says Earhart.

91
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,281
It's a strong theory with a lot of maybes.

92
00:06:38,681 --> 00:06:39,652
Theory three.

93
00:06:39,872 --> 00:06:45,222
They came down in the Marshall Islands, then controlled by Japan, and were captured.

94
00:06:45,722 --> 00:06:51,236
This one has stories from local witnesses and some supporters, but no hard documents.

95
00:06:51,736 --> 00:06:58,938
In twenty seventeen, a TV documentary showed an old photo said to show Earhart in the Marshall Islands.

96
00:06:59,158 --> 00:07:04,856
Within days, a blogger found the same photo in a travel book published in nineteen thirty five.

97
00:07:05,076 --> 00:07:06,897
Two years before she disappeared.

98
00:07:07,397 --> 00:07:12,440
Hard to be captured in nineteen thirty seven in a photo from nineteen thirty five.

99
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:15,600
So here's the honest version.

100
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:17,076
Documented.

101
00:07:17,296 --> 00:07:23,204
Earhart and Noonan left Lae on July second, nineteen thirty seven, for Howland Island.

102
00:07:23,424 --> 00:07:25,910
The Itasca heard her but she didn't hear them.

103
00:07:26,130 --> 00:07:32,436
Her last calls said fuel was low and placed her on the one five seven, three three seven line.

104
00:07:32,656 --> 00:07:34,983
No wreckage has ever been confirmed.

105
00:07:35,483 --> 00:07:36,213
Legend.

106
00:07:36,433 --> 00:07:43,850
Secret spy missions, faked deaths, and a new life under another name, none of which has any solid evidence.

107
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:54,918
What probably happened is that two very skilled people, flying an incredibly hard route, missed a tiny island by a small margin, and ran out of time.

108
00:07:56,318 --> 00:07:57,535
So what do you think?

109
00:07:57,755 --> 00:08:00,577
Deep ocean, Nikumaroro, or something else?

110
00:08:01,077 --> 00:08:02,325
Tell me in the comments.

111
00:08:02,545 --> 00:08:07,124
Bonus points if you can find Howland Island on a map without zooming in.

112
00:08:07,344 --> 00:08:08,100
I couldn't.

113
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:12,744
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

114
00:08:12,964 --> 00:08:17,384
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
